recommendations.text:
Treat this as the last decision year. No later round exists to gate, re-test or cancel a launch, so an A350 Re-engine ordered now is an unconditional commitment through its 2055 entry into service. The proof must therefore come first. If it is launched:
- the Audit Committee (chair Gemkow) runs an annual cash gate at $1.0B a year over 2050-54;
- the write-off schedule is $5B x years elapsed / 5;
- cancel only if a 787 Re-engine enters service first and the remaining spend loses at least $1B. A 787 Re-engine launched in 2050 enters service in 2055 at the earliest, so no Boeing move can meet that condition, and the case must stand on its own.

Safety, durability and the NGSA line. The twice-yearly safety review should take three reports as one package:
- Wagner's NGSA line report;
- the A320neo transition report;
- the in-service durability record of the CFM ducted and P&W-RR Joint Venture engines on NGSA (time on wing, life-limited parts, shop visits).
The Joint Venture watch item stays on the agenda: two of the three makers ride on one engine, and CFM is the backstop within resized needs. A flag from any engine maker comes to the Board before it reaches the cash line. It is answered through supply and fleet action, never by an NGSA re-timing. Widebody engineering draws on neither NGSA in-service support nor the A320neo transition.

Returns and rating. The CFO should show:
- the payout within the 30-50% policy;
- buyback room under the 18-month AGM authority, sized after the year's programme cash, with the A350 Re-engine first if it is launched;
- the base case beside the stress case;
- the A+/A1 headroom proxy.
There is to be no equity raise and no dividend cut outside a crisis. State support stays upside only and is never assumed in the case.

Integrity and the briefing objective. The 60/40 edge is not met: narrowbody share is 53% in 2050, on the long-run split, and no order on this menu moves it. The Board will not trade value or integrity for it. The ECSC should report the outcome of its review:
- each supply agreement resized to Airbus's own needs (1,060 a year from 2049), never held at the 2039 peak, with any agreement over EUR 300m brought to the Board;
- hiring that never targets rival teams;
- pricing within ordinary competition.
Delay Tactics stay none, and no unpriced move may recreate them.

frame.question:
It is 2050, the last decision year of the game. Do we keep the A350 at Do Nothing, the company default? That row is +23.37 in S1 and +23.33 in S2, with no Board item. Or do we launch the A350 Re-engine for entry into service in 2055? That row is +23.94 / +22.92, so the item alone is worth +0.57 in S1 and -0.41 in S2. No later round exists in which to gate, re-test or cancel it, so a launch now is an unconditional commitment. To launch, it must pass all of these:
- the five pillars, now including the in-service durability of NGSA's engines;
- the company's $1B launch test;
- Toepfer's added-move gate;
- Wagner's gates: an engine ready by entry into service, the A350F and Spirit A350 packages, and the ring-fence;
- a Qualified Majority of the Board.

levers_in_play:
Other moves, not priced on the board.
- Continue:
  - the twice-yearly safety review package;
  - supply agreements resized down to our own needs (1,060 a year from 2049), with any agreement over EUR 300m brought to the Board;
  - the Joint Venture watch item, with CFM as backstop;
  - open European hiring that never targets rival teams;
  - zero-for-zero trade advocacy [AX-0088];
  - returns inside the 30-50% payout and the AGM buyback authority, sized after programme cash.
- Close out:
  - the staged widebody case, recorded gate by gate;
  - research and technology for the generation after NGSA, and A350 family studies, kept as staged studies inside the Board-approved R&D budget, each below EUR 300m until a Board gate [A-0298, AG-0039]. None is presented as a launch.

red_lines:
Rules 4 and 5: Delay Tactics are closed, and none is the only value. No unpriced move may do by other means what the Board refused:
- supply agreements are resized to our own needs and never held at the peak;
- hiring never targets rival teams;
- pricing stays within ordinary competition.
Delay Tactics are never named in public.

The Board's would-veto list, carried into this round. It vetoes an A350 Re-engine launch:
- where my expected column is S2, or my S2 probability is 58% or more;
- carried by an override of a Toepfer or Wagner soft veto;
- carrying a binding ExCo veto I neither respected nor overrode as the rule allows, or a red-line flag I neither struck nor rebutted;
- that Wagner fails on safety, quality, the NGSA or A320neo ramp shield, or a supply crunch, including an engine-durability flag on the NGSA fleet that the launch would draw on.
It also vetoes any Delay Tactics value other than none, and any NGSA cancel.

Capital:
- the $5.2B line;
- the 30-50% payout;
- buybacks within the AGM authority, sized after the year's programme cash;
- no equity raise and no dividend cut outside a crisis;
- state money upside only [AX-0022, AX-0001].

Disclosure:
- no internal long-term-plan targets [AX-0005];
- windows, not dates, and no window the engine cannot meet;
- no widebody step tied to Boeing;
- never Delay Tactics;
- the Board vets the text [AX-0004].

tests.numbers:
My proposal (inference; the record gives no study sizes). Each item is staged with committed spend below €300m (about $0.33B) until a Board gate:
(1) research and technology for the generation after NGSA, inside the Board-approved R&D budget (FY2025 R&D €3.15bn); it is the item most likely to cross €300m in aggregate, and it comes to the Board when it does;
(2) A350 family studies;
(3) an NGSA derivative study;
(4) an A220 variant study.
Any launch would cross €300m, and above €800m it needs a Qualified Majority. Aggregate cap about €1.2bn (about $1.3B) over the study phase, roughly $0.35B a year: about 7% of the line with plan A, and about 26% ($1.35B) with plan B. None is presented as a launch.

B is the only launch this round. NGSA development ran 2030-37, and an A350 Re-engine would run 2050-55. The overlap is 0 years, so the strain is $0. The cash, $1.0B a year over 2050-54, is Toepfer's line.

Can a named widebody team staff it? On the board, yes. Off the board, talent binds [A-0366, A-0485], the record has no headcount, and I cannot name the team from the record. B therefore passes only on conditions:
- a named widebody team, ring-fenced from NGSA in-service support and the A320neo transition;
- the studies (an NGSA derivative, an A220 variant, research and technology for the next generation) stay studies, each below EUR 300m;
- at the supplier, RR confirms that the widebody engine work draws nothing from Joint Venture fleet support.

Launched in 2050, the A350 Re-engine enters service in 2055 (launch + 5).

The engine:
- Rolls-Royce is the A350's incumbent with the Trent XWB [A-0253]. It says there is 'no new widebody engine spend without a Re-engine'.
- UltraFan continues as technology only, with no product launch and no ready date.
- An UltraFan WB launched alongside us in 2050 would be ready in 2056 (launch + 6), one year late.

There is no fallback on the record:
- no Trent XWB derivative with a ready date;
- the Trent durability work (about GBP 1bn over four years) is time on wing for the existing fleet, not a new engine;
- the GEnx upgrade serves the 787;
- the CFM ducted, CFM-Embraer and Joint Venture engines are narrowbody engines;
- Boeing's own record finds no matured widebody engine for a 2050 entry into service.

Readiness therefore fails again, on the same one-year gap as in 2045 (entry into service in 2050, engine in 2051). The gap is built into the clocks: the engine at launch + 6, the airframe at launch + 5. It closes only if the engine is committed a year ahead of the airframe.

Grid margin against the best option that passes (A, hold): +0.57 in S1 and -0.41 in S2, under $1B in both columns.

